---
import {
  MCP_RELEASE,
  NOMUE_GLOBAL_INSTALL_COMMAND,
  NOMUE_NPX_COMMAND,
  NOMUE_VERIFY_COMMAND,
  PUBLIC_RELEASE,
} from '../../data/site-facts';
import PublicationLayout from '../../layouts/PublicationLayout.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: 'available', label: 'What is available' },
  { id: 'run', label: 'Install and run' },
  { id: 'package-path', label: 'Tested package path' },
  { id: 'local-boundary', label: 'Local verification boundary' },
  { id: 'release-status', label: 'Release status and scope' },
];
---

<PublicationLayout
  category="News"
  type="Product"
  title="nomue verifier Release 1 candidate is available on npm"
  description="The public @licklider/nomue-verifier package provides a direct local command for checking supported nomue Release 1 Records."
  published="September 1, 2026"
  status="Public release candidate — npm latest 0.2.1-rc.0"
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="available">
    <h2>What is available</h2>
    <p>
      <a href={PUBLIC_RELEASE.npmUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><code>{PUBLIC_RELEASE.npmPackage}</code></a>
      {' '}is publicly available on npm at version <code>{PUBLIC_RELEASE.verifierPackage}</code>.
      It exposes the <code>nomue</code> command for checking a local Record under the
      exact nomue Protocol Release 1 support target.
    </p>
    <p>
      The package turns the public verifier from a source checkout into a direct tool
      path: install the command globally, or invoke the same package through{' '}
      <code>npx</code> when a global install is not wanted. Source and release evidence
      remain available in the <a href={PUBLIC_RELEASE.verifierUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">public GitHub repository</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="run">
    <h2>Install and run</h2>
    <pre><code>{NOMUE_GLOBAL_INSTALL_COMMAND}
{NOMUE_VERIFY_COMMAND}</code></pre>
    <p>Or run the package without installing a global command:</p>
    <pre><code>{NOMUE_NPX_COMMAND}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Replace <code>./record.json</code> with the path to a conforming nomue Record and
      inspect the returned JSON report for the individual scoped outcomes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="package-path">
    <h2>Tested package path</h2>
    <p>
      The package path has passed CI on Linux, macOS, and Windows with Node.js 20 and
      22. Release checks exercise global installation, <code>nomue verify</code>, and the
      npm-exec-equivalent <code>npx</code> path rather than testing only the repository's
      internal source layout.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="local-boundary">
    <h2>Local verification boundary</h2>
    <p>
      npm must first obtain the package and its dependencies. After installation, Record
      verification runs on the local machine and does not call a nomue server. The
      verifier also does not fetch Record-supplied URIs, load remote schemas, or execute
      Record-supplied code.
    </p>
    <p>
      This makes the public package an independently runnable verification surface. It
      is not an announcement of a hosted nomue API or remote HTTP MCP endpoint. A
      separate local stdio adapter is now available as{' '}
      <a href={MCP_RELEASE.npmUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><code>{MCP_RELEASE.package}@{MCP_RELEASE.version}</code></a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="release-status">
    <h2>Release status and exact scope</h2>
    <div class="notice">
      <p>
        npm's <code>{PUBLIC_RELEASE.npmDistTag}</code> dist-tag currently points to{' '}
        <code>{PUBLIC_RELEASE.verifierPackage}</code>. The package is a release candidate,
        not a stable release.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p>
      The sole Release 1 bundle supported by this package is
      {' '}<code>{PUBLIC_RELEASE.bundle}</code>. It covers {PUBLIC_RELEASE.profile} under the{' '}
      {PUBLIC_RELEASE.procedure}. Other procedures and nearby bundle versions are not
      implied by installation or by the npm dist-tag.
    </p>
    <p>
      A clean report means that the named checks passed under that exact scope. It does
      not establish that the source data or researcher declarations are true, that the
      study is scientifically valid overall, or that a scientific conclusion is true.
      See the <a href={PUBLIC_RELEASE.protocolUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">nomue Protocol</a>{' '}
      for the authoritative Record and verification semantics.
    </p>
  </section>
</PublicationLayout>
